B1  Is the BALL doing the bending, or the order of the primes?  The twin chain is walked in the ball exactly as on the page
    (step = ratio of one twin start to the next, along the arm of its end digit).  Then the same steps are walked in shuffled order.
    Kill for -the order of the primes bends-: the real chain ends inside the middle 95 percent of the shuffled chains.
     600 pairs: path length 8.229 ; flat reader end 4.792 (any order) ; ball end, real order 4.858 ; shuffled 4.865 to 5.064  -> OUTSIDE: the order matters
    2400 pairs: path length 9.941 ; flat reader end 5.773 (any order) ; ball end, real order 5.848 ; shuffled 5.852 to 6.074  -> OUTSIDE: the order matters
    9600 pairs: path length 11.601 ; flat reader end 6.728 (any order) ; ball end, real order 6.806 ; shuffled 6.803 to 7.033  -> inside: the order adds nothing here

B2  Is the end-digit record flat?  Consecutive twin starts end in 1, 7 or 9.  If each end digit were drawn on its own, every
    cell below would read 1.000.  Kill for -the end digits carry a covariance-: all nine cells within 1 percent of 1.
   twin pairs counted: 239099
   from 1  to 1: 0.9196   to 7: 1.0948   to 9: 0.9858
   from 7  to 1: 0.9945   to 7: 0.9125   to 9: 1.0934
   from 9  to 1: 1.0864   to 7: 0.9925   to 9: 0.9206
   largest departure from 1: 9.5 percent ; one-sigma counting noise about 0.62 percent -> NOT flat

B3  Do the zeros behave like steps that commute (free to sit anywhere) or steps that do not (they push each other apart)?
    Share of neighbouring gaps smaller than 0.3 of the average: 26 percent if free, about 3 percent if they push.
    Kill for -the zeros push-: share of 15 percent or more.
   zeros used: 400 ; share of small gaps 1.0 percent -> they push
